---
title: Control and routing
description: Drive your audio with keyboard shortcuts, MIDI and logic - push to talk, mute toggles and scene switching in Splitwave.
order: 3
---

Control nodes carry **on/off signals** instead of audio. Their cables are dashed and their sockets are diamonds. A routing node such as **Mute**, **If**, **Switch** or **Select** takes those signals and decides where the audio goes.

## Sources of control

- **Key Input**: a global keyboard shortcut or a MIDI note or control. It is high while the key is held, even when Splitwave is in the background.
- **Level Trigger**: high while an audio input is louder than a threshold, held briefly after it drops so speech does not flicker it.
- **Timer**: in **Delay** mode it turns on a set time after its start input goes high; in **Repeat** mode it pulses at that interval while start stays high.

## Shaping control

- **Toggle**: flips its output each time the input goes high and keeps it until the next press. Turns a held key into an on/off switch.
- **Cycle**: steps through 2 or more outputs one press at a time and wraps around.
- **Logic**: combines signals with NOT, AND, OR, XOR and their inverses.

## Routing audio

- **Mute**: its **open** input owns the mute while connected. High lets audio through, low silences it.
- **If**: sends audio to its **true** output while the condition is high and to **false** otherwise, crossfading between them.
- **Switch**: one input, 2 to 8 outputs, each output with its own trigger.
- **Select**: 2 to 8 inputs, one output, each input with its own trigger.

See [Push to talk and mute shortcuts](/wiki/control/push-to-talk) for complete recipes.
